# Carrier Wound #24: The Concurrency Cap Counted Corpses As Live Owners

Wound #23 (recovery-machinery that survives the fire) shipped. The next dream
re-drive did not block with a blind marker — it blocked with something new, and
worse, because it was **predictable on a clock**: every fresh run died ~29 s in,
at planner admission, with `concurrency-cap-full`. Not sometimes. Every time.
A cap that is supposed to make work *wait* had quietly become a cap that makes
work *die*, and the live admin feed showed why: **38 `blocked` runs were still
squatting lane-owner slots** they had no living process behind.

This is the resource-leak wound in its cleanest form, and — like every wound
since [[carrier-wound-btoa-latin1-ceiling-2026-06-14]] — it was only visible
because the run was watched node-by-node from the outside, not stared at.

## Mechanism — the slot outlives the run that owned it

The supervisor Durable Object (`cloudflare-capsule-supervisor.ts`) is keyed by
`idFromName(workItemId)`. **Every dream run shares one workItemId**, so they all
land in **one** DO with **one** `record.activeLaneOwners` map
(`Record<laneId, runId>`) governed by **one** cap —
`WORKFLOW_APP_MAX_ACTIVE_LANES: "3"`. Admission is the gate (`admitLaneCore`):

```ts
if (activeLaneIds.length >= input.maxActiveLanes) {
  return AgentLaneAdmissionDecisionSchema.parse({
    ...,
    reason: "concurrency-cap-full",
    status: "deferred",
  });
}
```

The cap is only ever correct if `activeLaneOwners` holds **live** owners. It
doesn't, because **only one actor ever removes an owner**: the reaper, via
`releaseAdmissionSlotsAfterReap`, and **only for runs the reaper itself just
transitioned**. Two paths reach a terminal status *without* the reaper ever
touching them, and both leak the slot:

1. **Self-termination.** A run that blocks or captures on its own
   (`blocked`/`captured`) is never swept — the reaper only looks at runs
   `status NOT IN (terminal)`. The owner row in `activeLaneOwners` is never
   deleted.
2. **Record retirement by the driver.** `driveQueuedRuns` retires a run's
   run-start record the moment D1 reports it terminal. That deletes the
   *driver's* bookkeeping — it never reaches into the supervisor DO's owner map.

Either way the lane-owner slot squats forever with a dead runId behind it. Lane
ids are run-scoped (`lane:planner:<runId>`), so a leaked slot can **never** be
reused by the run that leaked it — it is pure dead weight. Three leaks and the
cap of three is permanently full. The 4th run, and every run after it, dies at
planner admission. 38 squatters was just how many had piled up before anyone
watched a run die on the clock.

> Site Reliability Engineering: How Google Runs Production Systems
> (Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy — O'Reilly, 2016), Ch. 23, "Quorum Leases":
>
> > The lease is for a specific (usually brief) period of time. Any operation
> > that changes the state of that data must be acknowledged by all replicas in
> > the read quorum. If any of these replicas becomes unavailable, the data
> > cannot be modified until the lease expires.

The whole reason a lease has a **bounded lifetime tied to liveness** is exactly
this: an owner that becomes unavailable must not be able to hold its grant
forever. Our admission slot was a lease with **no expiry and no liveness check**
— grant on admit, release only if one specific reaper happens to transition the
owner. Every other way an owner could die left the lease held by a corpse. The
fix gives the lease the missing liveness bound: *the authoritative run store is
the lease clock — a terminal owner has, by definition, let its lease lapse.*

This is also the [[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]] discipline applied to our own
state: the owner map politely *assumed* its entries were alive. The hostile
truth is that the run store is the only authority on liveness, and the map must
be reconciled against it before its count is trusted.

## Fix — reconcile owners against the run store before trusting the count

A single mechanism-agnostic reclaim, wired at **both** the gate and the clock.
It does not care HOW a slot leaked — only whether the owner is still alive.

```ts
private async reclaimTerminalOwnerSlots(
  record: SupervisorRecord
): Promise<SupervisorRecord> {
  const d1 = this.env.WORKFLOW_APP_D1;
  if (d1 === undefined) { return record; }                 // no authority ⇒ no-op
  const ownerRunIds = [...new Set(Object.values(record.activeLaneOwners))];
  if (ownerRunIds.length === 0) { return record; }

  const statusReader = createCloudflareWorkflowRunStatusReader({ d1 });
  const terminalRunIds = new Set<string>();
  for (const runId of ownerRunIds) {
    const snapshot = await statusReader.read({ runId });
    if (snapshot !== null && TERMINAL_RUN_STATES.has(snapshot.status)) {
      terminalRunIds.add(runId);                            // explicitly terminal ⇒ reclaim
    }
  }
  if (terminalRunIds.size === 0) { return record; }
  // drop terminal owners' lanes; append them to failedLaneIds (force-released ledger)
  ...
}
```

Two non-negotiable rules make this safe:

- **`null` snapshot is ALIVE, not dead.** A run with no authoritative row yet is
  in-flight — `read({runId})` returns `null`. We KEEP its slot. Freeing a slot on
  *absence of evidence* would yank a live run's reservation mid-flight. We free
  ONLY on an explicit terminal status. Absence is never terminal.
- **No D1 binding ⇒ no-op.** Without an authority to judge liveness, leave the
  record untouched. Better a stuck cap (visible, recoverable) than a wrongly
  freed slot (a live run silently losing its lane).

### Wired at the gate (spiral-breaker)

`admitLaneCore` reclaims, then **re-checks**, before deferring. A cap is only
real backpressure if it is still full *after* corpses are swept:

```ts
if (activeLaneIds.length >= input.maxActiveLanes) {
  record = await this.reclaimTerminalOwnerSlots(record);   // sweep dead owners
  activeLaneIds = activeLaneIdsOf(record);
}
if (activeLaneIds.length >= input.maxActiveLanes) {        // STILL full of LIVE owners?
  return /* deferred concurrency-cap-full */;              // only now is it backpressure
}
```

### Wired at the clock (safety net)

`alarm()`, after the reaper's own `releaseAdmissionSlotsAfterReap`, sweeps the
owner map unconditionally — so a jammed cap drains on the periodic alarm even
when **no** admission is currently probing (the case that produced the 38-run
pileup: nothing new was being admitted *because* the cap was jammed, so the
admission-time reclaim would never have fired on its own).

```ts
await this.reclaimTerminalOwnerSlots(await this.getRecord());
```

The two wirings are complements: the gate breaks the spiral the instant a new
run arrives; the clock drains a cap that nothing is currently knocking on.

## Chaos Receipts (would have caught it)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-run-reaper.test.ts` — **16 passing** (4 new + 1
corrected). The fake D1's status-reader query is answered from the same `runs`
fixture the reaper reads, so a run's terminal status is consistent across both
the reaper sweep and the liveness probe — no politeness gap between them.

- **"reclaims leaked terminal-owner slots so a fresh run is admitted instead of
  dying at a jammed cap"** — three runs (`blocked`/`blocked`/`captured`) squat
  the cap; a fresh run hits admission; asserts it is **admitted** (not deferred)
  and the three leaked lanes land in `failedLaneIds`. This is the production
  death spiral, reproduced: before the fix the fresh run defers
  `concurrency-cap-full` forever.
- **"does NOT reclaim a cap full of LIVE owners: genuine backpressure still
  defers"** — three `executing` owners; newcomer is deferred
  `concurrency-cap-full`; **nothing** is freed. The fix must not dissolve real
  backpressure.
- **"keeps the slot of an owner with NO authoritative row (in-flight): absence is
  not terminal"** — `runs: []` so every probe returns `null`; newcomer deferred;
  all three slots retained. Encodes the absence-is-alive rule as a test, not a
  comment.
- **"alarm safety net sweeps leaked terminal-owner slots with no admission
  probing"** — two terminal owners, a bare `alarm()` with nothing knocking; both
  slots drain to `failedLaneIds`. This is the 38-run pileup's exit path.
- **(corrected) "alarm reclaims a terminal run's leaked slot without re-reaping
  the run"** — was "alarm leaves a terminal run and its slot untouched", which
  asserted the leak *as correct* (`activeLaneIds === ["lane-a"]`). That assertion
  WAS the bug. Now: reaper does not re-reap the already-terminal run
  (`d1.events` empty) **and** the safety net reclaims its leaked slot
  (`activeLaneIds` empty, `failedLaneIds === ["lane-a"]`). An honest correction
  of an invariant that encoded the wound, not test-gaming.

The doubles are hostile by construction: a polite test would have pre-seeded
only live owners and never produced a corpse to count. Each new test
deliberately constructs the leaked-corpse state the production cap actually got
into.

## Verification

```bash
pnpm vitest run tests/integration/cloudflare-run-reaper.test.ts  # 16 pass (4 new, 1 corrected)
pnpm typecheck   # clean (tsgo)
pnpm check       # oxfmt + oxlint + type-aware, clean
```

## Known follow-up — Defect 2 (backpressure forged terminal)

The reclaim makes the cap honest about *liveness*, but a separate wound remains:
when the cap is genuinely full of live owners, the retryable
`concurrency-cap-full` / `already-active` deferral is forged into a **terminal**
`adapter_unavailable` in `proposeDynamicWorkflowBlueprint` (workflow-app.ts).
**The whole point of a concurrency cap is to make work WAIT, not DIE.** Fixing it
needs a `paused`-at-planning seam threaded through the critical `run()` path
(pause + re-arm instead of terminal-fail), which is a deeper change. Until then
the burn-in runs **serially** (submit one, wait for terminal) so live contention
never fires this path. Required before any *concurrent* burn-in. Tracked.

## Burn-In Gate

Per the Phase 2 D3 acceptance bar, the 10-run count stays at **zero**. This fix
deploys, the leaked owner slots GC on the next alarm / first admission, and ONE
dream-shape run is then watched node-by-node to `captured` on the fixed,
deployed code. Only then does D3 reset to zero and the serial 10-run burn-in
begin. This is the sibling of [[carrier-wound-node-recovery-oom-2026-06-14]]:
#23 made the announcer survive the fire; #24 makes admission control stop
counting the dead among the living. Every carrier wound this week surfaced from
node-by-node observation — *"feels like we should be able to observe the run in
progress and not guess if it's functioning instead of waiting and staring at it
like fucking dummies."* Captured on deployed code → fire the operator Discord
ping with the wzrrd HITL URL, reset D3, start the burn-in.
